# Difference between `emplace_back` and `push_back`

Both `emplace_back` and `push_back` are member functions of `std::vector` that add an element to the end of the vector. However, they differ in **how** they add the element.

## `push_back`

`push_back` adds a copy (or move) of an **already constructed** object to the vector.

- When you call `push_back`, you must provide a fully constructed object.
- The object is then **copied** (or **moved**) into the vector's storage.

## `emplace_back`

`emplace_back` **constructs** a new object directly in the vector's storage, in place.

- When you call `emplace_back`, you pass the **arguments** needed to construct the object, not the object itself.
- The object is constructed **in-place** at the end of the vector, avoiding unnecessary copies or moves.

## Code Example

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>

class Person {
public:
    std::string name;
    int age;

    Person(const std::string& name, int age) : name(name), age(age) {
        std::cout << "Constructed: " << name << "\n";
    }

    Person(const Person& other) : name(other.name), age(other.age) {
        std::cout << "Copied: " << name << "\n";
    }

    Person(Person&& other) noexcept : name(std::move(other.name)), age(other.age) {
        std::cout << "Moved: " << name << "\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    std::vector<Person> people;

    std::cout << "--- Using push_back ---\n";
    people.push_back(Person("Alice", 30));  // Constructs, then moves

    std::cout << "\n--- Using emplace_back ---\n";
    people.emplace_back("Bob", 25);  // Constructs in-place, no copy or move

    return 0;
}
```

