Here are the steps to fix this issue:

1. **Make sure your filter properly implements the `init` method**:

```java
public class YourFilter implements Filter {
    private FilterConfig filterConfig;

    @Override
    public void init(FilterConfig filterConfig) throws ServletException {
        this.filterConfig = filterConfig;
        // Other initialization code
    }

    // Other methods...
}
```

2. **Check your web.xml configuration** to ensure the filter is properly defined:

```xml
<filter>
    <filter-name>YourFilter</filter-name>
    <filter-class>com.yourpackage.YourFilter</filter-class>
</filter>
<filter-mapping>
    <filter-name>YourFilter</filter-name>
    <url-pattern>/*</url-pattern>
</filter-mapping>
```

3. **If you're using Servlet 3.0+, you can use annotations** instead of web.xml:

```java
@WebFilter("/*")
public class YourFilter implements Filter {
    // Implementation
}
```

4. **Add null checks** before using `getFilterConfig()`:

```java
public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, FilterChain chain) 
        throws IOException, ServletException {
    FilterConfig config = getFilterConfig();
    if (config != null) {
        String path = config.getServletContext().getRealPath("/") + "WEB-INF/classes/";
        // Use the path
    } else {
        // Handle the case when filterConfig is null
        // Maybe use an alternative way to get the path
    }
    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}
```

5. **Alternative approach** - Get the ServletContext directly from the request:

```java
public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, FilterChain chain) 
        throws IOException, ServletException {
    String path = request.getServletContext().getRealPath("/") + "WEB-INF/classes/";
    // Use the path
    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}
```

The last approach (number 5) is often the most reliable since it doesn't depend on the filter being properly initialized

