The matrix is guaranteed to be square (N x N).</think>To transpose a square matrix in-place, you only need to swap the elements across the main diagonal. The main diagonal consists of elements where the row index equals the column index (i.e., `matrix[i][i]`). 

For any element above the main diagonal at `matrix[i][j]` (where `i < j`), you swap it with its corresponding element below the main diagonal at `matrix[j][i]`. You must be careful not to swap elements below the main diagonal, as they have already been swapped, otherwise you will just revert the matrix back to its original state.

Here is the C++ implementation:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <utility> // For std::swap

void transpose(std::vector<std::vector<int>>& matrix) {
    int n = matrix.size();

    // Iterate through each row
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        // Iterate through the columns starting from i + 1 
        // to only target the upper triangle (above the main diagonal)
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; ++j) {
            // Swap the element with its counterpart across the diagonal
            std::swap(matrix[i][j], matrix[j][i]);
        }
    }
}
```

### Complexity
- **Time Complexity:** $\mathcal{O}(N^2)$ because we visit roughly half of the elements in the $N \times N$ matrix to swap them.
- **Space Complexity:** $\mathcal{O}(1)$ because the transposition is done strictly in-place using only a temporary variable for swapping (handled internally by `std::swap`).在C++中，`std::swap` 是如何工作的？它和手写交换逻辑有什么区别？

